          "turn_index": 0,
          "type": "turn",
          "question": "At what blood pressure reading is hypertension diagnosed, and do the major bodies agree?",
          "answer": "A diagnosis of hypertension is not based on a single blood pressure reading; it requires an average of two or more readings taken on two or more separate occasions (Major cardiology society consensus guidance, educational summary: Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets).\n\nMajor medical bodies do not entirely agree on the diagnostic thresholds for hypertension (guideline-type-2-diabetes-screening, guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets, guideline-anxiety-screening-and-first-line-care, guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation). There is some disagreement among major bodies regarding specific thresholds (Major cardiology society consensus guidance, educational summary: Hypertension: diagnostic thresholds and blood-pressure targets).\n\nIn the United States, hypertension is diagnosed at a blood pressure reading of 130/80 mm Hg or higher, according to the Major cardiology society consensus guidance (guideline-type-2-diabetes-screening, guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets, guideline-anxiety-screening-and-first-line-care, guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation). However, European guidance considers office hypertension to begin at 140/90 mm Hg (guideline-type-2-diabetes-screening, guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets, guideline-anxiety-screening-and-first-line-care, guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation), and some guidance describes hypertension as beginning at 140/90 mm Hg (Major cardiology society consensus guidance, educational summary: Hypertension: when treatment starts and which options are first-line). European guidance classifies blood pressure readings between 130-139 / 80-89 mm Hg as \"high-normal blood pressure\" rather than Stage 1 hypertension (guideline-type-2-diabetes-screening, guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets, guideline-anxiety-screening-and-first-line-care, guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation). Therefore, a blood pressure reading can be labeled differently depending on whether US or European guidelines are applied (guideline-type-2-diabetes-screening, guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets, guideline-anxiety-screening-and-first-line-care, guideline-lipid-screening-and-statin-initiation).\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
